        "problem": "Solve the inequality $(2x-5)^{3}\\cdot {3}^{x+6}<0.$",
        "worked_solution": "When we have an inequality involving an exponential function with a positive base, we're allowed to multiply or divide both sides by the exponential function.\n\nDividing both sides of the given inequality by ${3}^{x+6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x-5)^{3}\\cdot {3}^{x+6} & <0 \\\\ \\frac{(2x-5)^{3}\\cdot {3}^{x+6}}{{3}^{x+6}} & <\\frac{0}{{3}^{x+6}} \\\\ (2x-5)^{3} & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the inequality using familiar methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{(2x-5)^{3}} & <\\sqrt[3]{0} \\\\ 2x-5 & <0 \\\\ 2x & <5 \\\\ x & <\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
